1.8 HTTP in Detail: Message Format
Both a request and a response follow the same general structure:

A first line, differing between a request (method, URL and HTTP version) and a response (HTTP version, status code and reason)
A set of headers, carrying metadata about the message (for instance, the destination host, the content type, or its length)
An optional body, holding the actual data

A GET request typically has no body: all the data it carries goes in the URL itself, as parameters. A POST request carries its data in the message body, outside the URL.

1.8.1 Example: From a Form to an HTTP Request
Consider a simple HTML form used to look up a country’s capital from its code:
&lt;form method="GET" action="/capital"&gt;
  &lt;label for="countrycode"&gt;Country code&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input type="text" id="countrycode" name="countrycode"&gt;
  &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Look up&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
When submitted with the value PT, the browser generates the following HTTP request:
GET /capital?countrycode=PT HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Because this is a GET request, the countrycode parameter is sent as part of the URL (after the ?), and there is no body in the message. If the same form used method="POST", the parameter would instead travel in the request body, and the URL would stay clean (/capital).
The server, upon processing this request, returns an HTTP response such as the following:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"country": "Portugal", "capital": "Lisboa", "ccode": "PT"}


1.8.2 Example: a POST Request with a Body
Consider now a login form, with the fields user and pass:
&lt;form method="POST" action="/login"&gt;
  &lt;label for="user"&gt;Username&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input type="text" id="user" name="user"&gt;
  &lt;label for="pass"&gt;Password&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input type="password" id="pass" name="pass"&gt;
  &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Log in&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
When submitted, this form generates the following HTTP request:
POST /login HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Content-Length: 21

user=javaee&pass=esigelec2026
Unlike the previous example, the parameters no longer travel in the URL: they follow in the request body, after a blank line separating the headers from the body. This is why credentials and other sensitive data are typically sent via POST, never GET: a request body is not recorded in browser history or in the server’s logs, unlike URL parameters.

&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;My Page&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;First version of the page.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;

&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Example Page&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Site Title&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/header&gt;
  &lt;nav&gt;&lt;!-- menu here --&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;
  &lt;main&gt;
    &lt;section&gt;
      &lt;h2&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Initial content.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/section&gt;
  &lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;footer&gt;© 2026&lt;/footer&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;

&lt;nav&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#home"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#about"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#contact"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
&lt;section&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Contact form&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;form&gt;
    &lt;label for="name"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input id="name" name="name" type="text"&gt;
    &lt;label for="email"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input id="email" name="email" type="email"&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Send&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

2.2 CSS: Three Ways to Apply Styles
The minimal HTML shown above only describes the page’s structure; visual presentation is the responsibility of CSS (Cascading Style Sheets), which can be attached to HTML in three distinct ways.
Inline, directly in an element’s style attribute:
&lt;h1 style="color:#0d6efd; margin:0.5rem 0;"&gt;Site Title&lt;/h1&gt;
Internal, in a &lt;style&gt; block inside &lt;head&gt;:
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;style&gt;
    :root { --brand:#0d6efd; }
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; line-height:1.5; }
    nav ul { list-style:none; display:flex; gap:1rem; padding:0; }
    nav a { text-decoration:none; padding:0.4rem 0.6rem; }
    nav a:hover { background:var(--brand); }
  &lt;/style&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
External, in a separate .css file, linked with &lt;link&gt;:
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
/* styles.css */
:root { --brand:#0d6efd; }
body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; line-height:1.5; }
nav ul { list-style:none; display:flex; gap:1rem; padding:0; }
nav a { text-decoration:none; padding:0.4rem 0.6rem; }
nav a:hover { background: var(--brand); }
Of the three, external CSS is generally the best practice: it clearly separates structure (HTML) from presentation (CSS), allows the same file to be reused across several pages, and keeps each document shorter and easier to read.

2.3 Classes, IDs and Specificity
To style groups of elements, CSS uses two main selectors: the class (class), which can repeat across several elements, and the identifier (id), which must be unique within the page.
&lt;h1 id="title" class="title"&gt;Site Title&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;nav class="menu"&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="menu__link active" href="#home"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="menu__link" href="#about"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="menu__link" href="#contact"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
#title { color: var(--brand); }               /* id: unique per page */
.menu { border-block: 1px solid #eee; padding: 0.75rem 0; }
.menu__link { text-decoration: none; padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem; }
.menu__link.active { font-weight: 600; }
When two CSS rules conflict over the same element, the winner is decided by specificity, in decreasing order:

!important (avoid it; makes CSS hard to maintain)
Inline style (style="...")
Identifier (#id)
Class (.class), attribute or pseudo-class
Element (h1, p, a, …)

h1 { color: black; }
.title { color: blue; }
#title { color: green; }  /* wins over both previous rules */

2.4 Bootstrap: a CSS Framework
Writing and maintaining an entire application’s CSS from scratch is a considerable amount of work, especially when the result also needs to be responsive (adapting to different screen sizes) and visually consistent. A CSS framework such as Bootstrap solves this by providing:

Productivity and visual consistency, through ready-made utility classes
Responsiveness already built in, through a grid and spacing classes
Tested, accessible components (forms, navigation bars, buttons, among others)

Bootstrap can be included directly via CDN (Content Delivery Network), with no installation needed: a stylesheet in &lt;head&gt;, and a JavaScript file (for the interactive components) before the closing &lt;/body&gt;.
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;!-- page content --&gt;
  &lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
A navigation bar (navbar), with Bootstrap, is ready in a handful of lines, already responsive (it collapses into a menu on narrow screens):
&lt;nav class="navbar navbar-expand-md bg-light border-bottom"&gt;
  &lt;div class="container"&gt;
    &lt;a class="navbar-brand" href="#"&gt;My Site&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;button class="navbar-toggler" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#nav1"&gt;
      &lt;span class="navbar-toggler-icon"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;div id="nav1" class="collapse navbar-collapse"&gt;
      &lt;ul class="navbar-nav ms-auto gap-2"&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="#home"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="#about"&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="#contact"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
A form gets the same visual treatment through the form-label and form-control classes:
&lt;section class="container my-4"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Contact form&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;form class="row g-3 col-lg-6"&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-12"&gt;
      &lt;label for="name" class="form-label"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;input id="name" name="name" type="text" class="form-control"&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-12"&gt;
      &lt;label for="email" class="form-label"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;input id="email" name="email" type="email" class="form-control"&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-12"&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit"&gt;Send&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
And the same grid system organises the main content into columns, which rearrange automatically depending on screen width (col-12 full width on small screens, col-md-8 eight of twelve columns from medium screens onward):
&lt;main class="container my-4"&gt;
  &lt;div class="row"&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-12 col-md-8"&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Main content…&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;aside class="col-12 col-md-4"&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Sidebar…&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/aside&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/main&gt;

2.6 Exercise: the Static Pages of the “World Capitals” Application
Applying what was presented in the previous sections, the visual pages of the application used throughout the practical LABs can now be built: an API that serves information about countries and their capitals. At this stage, the pages are entirely static, in HTML/CSS/Bootstrap, with no JavaScript at all: the data shown is fixed in the HTML itself, and the forms are not yet connected to any server. That is the work reserved for the LABs, which will simply add fetch() to this already-finished code.
All pages share the same navigation bar:
&lt;nav class="navbar navbar-expand-md bg-light border-bottom"&gt;
  &lt;div class="container"&gt;
    &lt;a class="navbar-brand" href="index.html"&gt;World Capitals&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;button class="navbar-toggler" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#nav1"&gt;
      &lt;span class="navbar-toggler-icon"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;div id="nav1" class="collapse navbar-collapse"&gt;
      &lt;ul class="navbar-nav ms-auto gap-2"&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="index.html"&gt;Countries&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="capital.html"&gt;Look Up Capital&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="countries-edit.html"&gt;Manage Countries&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="login.html"&gt;Log In&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
Building on this shared navbar, four pages are put together:

Country listing (index.html): a Bootstrap table, with a few countries fixed in the HTML as an example. In the LABs, this table is dynamically filled in from the result of /country.
Authentication (login.html): a form with the fields user and pass, using the same names the /login endpoint expects to receive.
Capital look-up (capital.html): a simple form, with a single countrycode field, matching the example already shown in Chapter 1.
Managing countries (countries-edit.html): three separate forms, one per operation, all pointing to the same /countryEdit endpoint, distinguished by the hidden operation field (see the note on this design in Chapter 6).

These four pages, linked together by the shared navigation bar, already form a small but complete and functional Web site, even without any server-side logic: it is possible to navigate between them, fill in the forms and submit them. What is still missing is a server to answer these requests, and that is what the following chapters provide.
The complete, standalone code for all four pages, ready to copy into an editor and open directly in the browser, is available in the Portuguese edition’s Appendix C (apendiceC.qmd), which does not yet have an English counterpart.
